  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Find the stationary inputs",
      "body": "<p>For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>, differentiate and factor: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>",
      "recap": "A derivative sign chart identifies where a polynomial changes direction.",
      "pitfall": "The zeros of the derivative are not automatically zeros of the original function.",
      "pause": "Find the three stationary inputs.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l19",
          "reminder": "A derivative sign chart identifies where a polynomial changes direction."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Find their heights",
      "body": "<p>Substitute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>. Compare each value with zero to see whether the middle part of the graph reaches the axis.</p>",
      "recap": "Turning heights, not just turning inputs, control the number of crossings.",
      "pitfall": "A local maximum can be negative.",
      "pause": "Find all three heights and the derivative sign on the intervals between them.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b3-l05",
          "reminder": "Turning heights, not just turning inputs, control the number of crossings."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Use both far ends",
      "body": "<p>The positive <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></math> term makes both ends rise without bound. The derivative signs are negative, positive, negative, positive across the four intervals.</p>",
      "recap": "Monotonic intervals and endpoint heights determine exactly how many crossings occur.",
      "pitfall": "Check the two outer intervals as well as the intervals between stationary points.",
      "pause": "Count the crossings in each monotonic interval.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b3-l05",
          "reminder": "Monotonic intervals and endpoint heights determine exactly how many crossings occur."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>12</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The stationary heights are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p>The derivative signs on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo>&#x0221E;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x0221E;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are respectively <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo></mrow></math>. The graph remains below zero throughout the middle two intervals. On the left it decreases from positive infinity to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>, crossing once; on the right it increases from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> to positive infinity, crossing once.</p><p>There are <strong>2 real solutions: C.</strong></p>",
